{
  "dom_id": "step-q162411",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14096233/assets/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=\u27e8{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y\u27e9$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\u222d}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2y)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={\u222d}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{\u222d}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
